  "cover_art_url": "https://is1-ssl.mzstatic.com/image/thumb/Music124/v4/f1/e1/2f/f1e12f95-4c4c-4374-805e-ccf84d49298f/00602547786968.rgb.jpg/600x600bb.jpg",
  "style_primary": "hard-bop",
  "style_tags": [],
  "rationale": "Quincy Jones's first big-band album as leader, and the record that made him a bandleader as well as an arranger: a New York studio orchestra playing the hard-bop songbook (Moanin', Along Came Betty, Whisper Not, I Remember Clifford, Whisper Not's Tickle Toe) with Ellington/Basie weight in the brass and the best young soloists of 1959 up front. AllMusic gives it 5 stars, Yanow calling it 'one of his finest jazz recordings' (S1, S2). Session gravity is the case: Clark Terry, Harry 'Sweets' Edison, Joe Newman, Ernie Royal and Joe Wilder in the trumpet section; Jimmy Cleveland, Urbie Green, Quentin Jackson and Melba Liston in the trombones; Phil Woods, Zoot Sims, Benny Golson, Frank Wess, Jerome Richardson and Sahib Shihab in the reeds; Patti Bown, Kenny Burrell, Milt Hinton and Sam Woodyard in the rhythm section over five 1959 sessions at Fine Recording (S1, S3). Lineage: this band is the bridge from the Basie/Ellington wing already in the canon to the arranger-led orchestras of the 1960s, and Quincy Jones is entirely absent from the canon today \u2014 a visible hole for a musician whose big-band writing sits directly on top of what is already collected. It still plays: 'Moanin'' and 'The Birth of a Band' swing hard, and the record is an easy way into why big-band writing mattered to this tradition. Style: big-band hard bop (the format is an orchestra; the material and the solo language are hard bop).",
  "priority": "strong",
  "epistemic": "obs",
