    "notes": "Five 1959 sessions at Fine Recording, New York \u2014 one of C. Robert Fine's two Manhattan facilities, in the former ballroom of the Great Northern Hotel at 118 West 57th Street (S5, S6). It is NOT the same room as 'Fine Sound Studios' at 711 Fifth Avenue, which is the other, earlier Fine studio already in the canon's place list; no existing slug matches this venue, so a new place is created. Session map per Lord's data (S3): 1959-02-09 (source says 'February 9 or 10') \u2014 Tuxedo Junction; 1959-03-09 ('March 9 and 10') \u2014 A Change of Pace; 1959-05-26 \u2014 Happy Faces; 1959-05-27 ('May 27 and 28') \u2014 Along Came Betty, I Remember Clifford, Whisper Not, The Gypsy, Tickle Toe; 1959-06-16 \u2014 Moanin', The Birth of a Band. Only one documented date is stored per session pair; the second day of each pair is unresolved in the sources. Quincy Jones conducted and arranged seven of the ten charts but played no instrument, so he carries no personnel row (the instrument taxonomy has no conductor). Arrangers: Jones (tracks 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 9, 10), Nat Pierce (3), Al Cohn (5), Melba Liston (8) per S1; Lord's session notes assign Pierce to I Remember Clifford, Cohn to Tickle Toe and Liston to The Gypsy, which agrees. No producer or engineer credit is printed on any source consulted (AllMusic credits tab lists performers and composers only; Wikipedia, Lord and Discogs are silent), so both are null with epistemic_production unk. Conflicts preserved: Discogs (S4) is a 2-LP reissue that also carries the separate 'Birth of a Band, Vol. 2' programme (November 1959 sessions, tracks C1-D5) \u2014 those tracks are excluded here, and its track order and its Moanin' credits (Hinton/Persip, May 26) disagree with Lord (Brown/Lamond, June 16); both recorded, June 16 track marked unk. Wikipedia omits 'The Midnight Sun Will Never Set', which appears only on the combined reissue and belongs to the Vol. 2 dates. Wikipedia lists 'Charlie Persip' among the album's drummers; he is carried here with unk and the conflict noted."
